## 3. Methodology & Execution
To begin with, I started by drawing a sort of "hub and spoke" drawing to enable me to visualize the routes and how they could be interconnected. 
I knew that utilizing the more expensive helicopter was out of the question if the goal was to keep costs down. After going through a few rounds of trial and error, I eventually determined an ideal route to take. With the main "X factor" being letting the trucks sit idle, which allowed for battery charging and loading at the depot to occur in one fell swoop. 
Which I then uploaded my findings to Claude to see if there were any shortcomings that I could have missed. Then it was a matter of representing it in a more clean, concise manner.


## 4. Challenges & Troubleshooting
Realistically, the biggest challenge came from simply ironing out a way that would deliver the vaccines unspoiled and within their proper windows. 
There were several rounds of an "Aww shoot! Gotta start over again!" draft that just did not work at all, despite me initally believing it would.
The final analysis with Claude was more or less making sure that our two parameters and guidelines were roughly homoousian.

## 5. Conclusion & Lessons Learned
All in all, this was a general success as I met all of the mission parameters and delivered the vaccines in an affordable, reliable manner. 
I believe that visualizing the delivery model aided greatly in my comprehension of the assignment and would have been lost in the sauce otherwise.
